  {
    "episode_id": 92704,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92704.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 35,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, let's see, I've made sugar cookies. I have filled little donuts. I am now eating cheesecake. I rang up some of the customers. They taught me how to run the, you know, you don't even have cash registers anymore. You have like, you know, they have an iPad one of those little things that you've run a credit card through. And what else did I do? Made up, filled up a bag of white wine cookies, which I thought was pretty original. It put boxes together. I was not very good at the boxes. I was very good at the sugar cookies. They said the sugar cookies were like among the most beautiful they've ever seen in their entire lives. That's a little bit of an exaggeration. But no, it was great. You've got two women. We were talking earlier about some of the challenges of even trying to expand and trying to move some things around and having to deal with the bureaucracy of government and what a hassle that was. You know, this is hard work owned in a business. And I have great admiration for people who give it a try. And I wish these women wonderful success. They've been for a number of years now. And in fact, if I'm ever running ahead of schedule when I pull into town, this is where I like to stop. Because they're good people, and they're hard working, and they make very good suites.",
